All the exercises use one theme, a Library system (books, members, loans), so each step builds on the last and ends as a Spring Boot app. Set up JDK 21 and IntelliJ IDEA Community first, and type the code yourself instead of copying it.

1. Classes + objects
Create a Book class with fields title, author, year, and a method describe() that prints them.
In main, create 3 Book objects and call describe() on each.
Create a Member class (name, email) and print two members.
2. public/private/protected + packages
Put your classes in packages: com.library.model (Book, Member) and com.library.app (Main).
Make all fields private, then add getters and setters. Try accessing book.title from Main and read the compiler error.
Add a setter for year that rejects values below 0.
3. Constructors + this
Add a constructor Book(String title, String author, int year) that uses this.
Add a second constructor Book(String title, String author) that calls the first one with this(...) and a default year.
Override toString() so System.out.println(book) prints nicely.
4. static + final
Add private static int count to Book and increment it in the constructor. Add static int getCount().
Create a LibraryConfig class with public static final int MAX_LOANS = 3.
Make the title field final, then see what breaks and why.
5. Inheritance + overriding
Create Ebook extends Book with an extra field fileSizeMb.
Call super(...) in its constructor and override describe() to add the file size.
Create a Book[] array holding both Book and Ebook objects and loop over it. Notice which describe() runs (polymorphism).
6. Abstract classes
Create abstract class Item with title and an abstract method double getLateFee(int days).
Make Book and Dvd extend Item, each with a different fee rule.
Try new Item() and read the error.
7. Interfaces
Create the interface Borrowable with borrow(), giveBack() and isAvailable().
Make Book implement it and keep an available flag.
Create a Printable interface with a default method, and implement both interfaces in one class.
8. Exceptions
Create a custom exception BookNotAvailableException extends Exception.
Make borrow() throw it when the book is already borrowed, and handle it with try/catch in main.
Write a method that parses a String to an int and catches NumberFormatException.
Use try/finally to print "Done" whatever happens.
9. ArrayList, HashMap, HashSet
Store 5 books in an ArrayList<Book>, then add, remove and loop over them.
Use a HashMap<String, Book> keyed by ISBN to look up a book. Try an ISBN that doesn't exist.
Add duplicate titles to a HashSet<String> and see what happens.
Build a LibraryService class that holds the list and has addBook, findByAuthor and removeBook.
10. Generics
Write a generic class Box<T> with set(T) and get(). Test it with String and Book.
Write a generic method static <T> T firstOf(List<T> list).
Write a generic interface Repository<T, ID> with save, findById and findAll, then implement BookRepository using a HashMap. Spring Data uses this same pattern.
11. Lambdas
Sort your books by year using list.sort((a, b) -> ...).
Use list.forEach(b -> System.out.println(b.getTitle())), then rewrite it with a method reference (System.out::println).
Use removeIf to delete all books older than 1990.
Write your own functional interface Validator<T> with boolean validate(T t) and pass a lambda to it.
12. Streams
filter: get all books by one author.
map: get a List<String> of titles in uppercase.
reduce: compute the sum of all page counts (add a pages field).
Combine them: titles of books published after 2000, sorted, joined into one string with Collectors.joining(", ").
groupingBy: group books by author into a Map<String, List<Book>>.
13. Maven
Create a Maven project (mvn archetype:generate or IntelliJ's "New Project → Maven").
Move your code into src/main/java and read the pom.xml: groupId, artifactId, version, dependencies.
Add a dependency (JUnit 5), write a test for LibraryService.findByAuthor in src/test/java, and run it with mvn test.
Run mvn clean package and find the .jar in target/.
14. Spring Boot

Generate a project at start.spring.io (Maven, Java 21, dependencies: Spring Web, Spring Data JPA, H2 Database).

Hello endpoint: create a @RestController with GET /hello that returns text.
Books API (in memory): GET /books, GET /books/{id}, POST /books, DELETE /books/{id}, backed by a plain List<Book>. Test with Postman or curl.
Layers: split the code into BookController → BookService (@Service) → BookRepository, and inject them through constructors. This is dependency injection, the core Spring idea.
Database: turn Book into a @Entity, make BookRepository extends JpaRepository<Book, Long>, and run it against H2. Add a custom method findByAuthor(String author).
Errors: throw your custom exception when a book isn't found and return a 404 using @ControllerAdvice or @ResponseStatus.
Validation: add @NotBlank on the title and @Valid on the request body.
